iliation: Simon Fraser University Affiliation: Canada    Rodrigo B. Platte Affiliation: School of Mathematical and Statistical Sciences Affiliation: Arizona State University Affiliation: USA    Alexei Shadrin Affiliation: DAMTP, Centre for Mathematical Sciences Affiliation: University of Cambridge Affiliation: United Kingdom\nL363: due to the assumptions on $d$. Hence this value of $K$ satisfies (cite82†3.12 ). We next apply (cite81†3.11 ), the bounds $K\\geq d\\nu/2$ and $K\\leq 2d\\nu$ and (cite83†3.13 ) to deduce that\nL364: \nL365:  | $$B(M,N)\\gtrsim\\nu^{-2}\\left(2^{d/2}\\right)^{\\nu}\\geq\\rho^{\\nu},$$  |\nL366: \nL367: for some $\\rho>1$. This holds for all $\\nu>2/d$. But since $d$ is a constant, we deduce that $B(M,N)\\gtrsim\\rho^{\\nu}$ for all $\\nu\\geq 1$. This completes the proof. ∎\nL368: This result shows that if $M=\\mathcal{O}\\left(N^{\\tau}\\right)$ for some $0<\\tau\\leq 2(\\gamma+1)$ then the maximal polynomial grows at least exponentially fast with rate\nL369: \nL370:  | $$r=\\frac{2(\\gamma+1)-\\tau}{2\\gamma+1}.$$  |\nL371: \nL372: In particular, the scaling $M\\asymp N^{2(\\gamma+1)}$, $N\\rightarrow\\infty$, is necessary for boundedness of the maximal polynomial. In §cite18†5.1 we will show that this rate is also sufficient.\nL373: It is informative to relate this result to several of the examples introduced in §cite12†2.4 . First, if $\\gamma=0$, i.e. case (U), we recover the lower bound of Theorem cite53†2.2 . Conversely, for (C2) we have\nL374: \nL375:  | $$B(M,N)\\geq C\\sigma^{\\sqrt{N^{3}/M}}.$$  |\nL376: \nL377: Thus, the cubic scaling $M\\asymp N^{3}$ is necessary for stability. Finally, for the points (UC) we have\nL378: \nL379:  | $$B(M,N)\\geq C\\sigma^{(M^{3/2}/N)^{2}},$$  |\nL380: which implies a necessary scaling of $M\\asymp N^{3/2}$. Note that Corollary cite84†3.2 says nothing about the case $-1<\\gamma\\leq-1/2$. We discuss the case $\\gamma=-1/2$ further in Remark cite15†3.2 .\nL381: \nL382: The case of linear oversampling ($M\\asymp N$) warrants closer inspection:\nL383: ###### Corollary 3.3.\nL384: \nL385: Let $\\mu(x)$, $C$ and $\\sigma$ be as in Corollary cite84†3.2 and $c\\geq 1$. Then\nL386: \nL387:  | $$B(\\lfloor cN\\rfloor,N)\\geq C\\left(\\sigma^{c^{-\\frac{1}{2\\gamma+1}}}\\right)^{N}.$$  |\nL388: \nL389: In particular, the Lebesgue constants $\\Lambda(N)=B(N,N)$ satisfy\nL390: \nL391:  | $$\\Lambda(N)\\geq C\\sigma^{N}.$$  |\nL392: (C2)  | (UC)  | (OC)\nL393: --- | --- | ---\nL394: cite85†Image: Refer to caption | cite86†Image: Refer to caption | cite87†Image: Refer to caption L395: Figure 4: The growth of $B(M,N)$ as a function of $M$ for the node densities (C2), (UC), and (OC). In each case $B(M,N)$ is plotted for $N=M$, $N=2/3M$, and $N=1/2M$.\nL396: In other words, whenever the points cluster more slowly than quadratically at one of the endpoints (recall that $\\gamma=\\max\\{\\alpha,\\beta\\}>-1/2$ in the above result), linear oversampling (including the case $M=N$, i.e. interpolation) necessarily leads to exponential growth of the maximal polynomial a geometric rate. Figure cite88†4 illustrates this for the cases (C2) and (UC).\nL397: Interestingly, the case (OC), although not covered by this result, also exhibits exponential growth, whenever the oversampling factor is below a particular threshold.\nL398: In this paper we are primarily interested in lower bounds for $B(M,N)$, since this is all that is required for the various impossibility theorems. However, in the case of ultraspherical weight functions an upper bound can be derived from results of Rakhmanov. Specifically, let $\\gamma=\\alpha=\\beta>-1/2$ and $x_{1},\\ldots,x_{M}$ be a set of $M$ points that are equispaced with respect to the ultraspherical weight function (cite89†2.10 ). Then [cite57†29 , Thm. 1(a)] gives that\nL399:  | $$\\max_{-r\\leq x\\leq r}|p(x)|\\leq C\\max\\{|p(x_{m})|,m=1,\\ldots,M\\},\\hskip 20.00003pt\\forall p\\in\\mathbb{P}_{N},$$  |\nL400: for some constant $C=C_{r}$ depending only on $r$, where $r^{2}<1-(N/M)^{\\tau}$ and $\\tau=\\frac{2}{2\\gamma+1}$^{1}^{1} 1 Rakhmanov’s result excludes the endpoints $x=\\pm 1$ from this set, whereas in our results we include these points. However as noted earlier, our main theorems would remain valid (with minor changes) if these points were excluded.. Remez’ inequality (see, for example, [cite90†11 , Thm. 5.1.1]) now gives that\nL401:  | $$\\max_{|x|\\leq 1}|p(x)|\\leq CT_{N}(1/r)\\max\\{|p(x_{m})|,m=1,\\ldots,M\\},\\hskip 20.00003pt\\forall p\\in\\mathbb{P}_{N},$$  |\nL402: \nL403: where $T_{N}$ is the $N^{\\mathrm{th}}$ Chebyshev polynomial. Suppose that $N/M\\leq 1/2$. Then\nL404: \nL405:  | $$T_{N}(1/r)<\\left(1/r+\\sqrt{1/r^{2}-1}\\right)^{N}<\\left(1+(N/M)^{\\tau}+2(N/M)^{\\tau/2}\\right)^{N}\\leq\\exp(c(N/M)^{\\tau/2}N),$$  |\nL406: \nL407: for some $c>0$. Using the definition of $\\tau$, we obtain\nL408:  | $$\\|p\\|_{\\infty}\\leq\\tilde{C}\\tilde{\\sigma}^{\\nu}\\max\\{|p(x_{m})|,m=1,\\ldots,M\\},\\hskip 20.00003pt\\forall p\\in\\mathbb{P}_{N},$$  |\nL409: \nL410: for $\\tilde{C}>0$ and $\\tilde{\\sigma}>1$, where\nL411: \nL412:  | $$\\nu=\\left(\\frac{N^{2(\\gamma+1)}}{M}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{2\\gamma+1}}.$$  |\nL413: The exponent $\\nu$ is exactly the same as in the lower bound for $B(M,N)$ (Corollary cite84†3.2 ). In other words, the two-sided bounds of Coppersmith & Rivlin (see Theorem cite53†2.2 ) for equispaced nodes extend to nodes equidistributed according to ultraspherical weight functions.\nL414: \nL415: Corollaries cite84†3.2 and cite91†3.3 do not apply to the nodes (C1). It is, however, well-known (see, for example, [cite52†31 , Thm. 15.2]) that\nL416: \nL417:  | $$\\Lambda(N)\\sim\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\log(N),\\hskip 10.00002ptN\\rightarrow\\infty,$$  |\nL484: ###### Corollary 4.3.\nL485: \nL486: Consider the setup of Theorem cite42†4.2 . If $\\gamma=\\max\\{\\alpha,\\beta\\}>-1/2$ then the following holds:\nL487: \nL488:   * (i)\nL489: \nL490: If $F_{M}(f)$ converges exponentially fast with geometric rate for all $f\\in B(E)$ (i.e. $\\tau=1$ in (cite95†4.2 )) then the condition numbers $\\kappa(F_{M})\\geq\\sigma^{M}$ grow exponentially fast with geometric rate.\nL491: \nL492:   * (ii)\nL493: \nL494: The best possible rate of exponential convergence of a stable method $F_{M}$ is subgeometric with index $\\frac{1}{2(\\gamma+1)}$.\nL495: ###### Proof.\nL496: \nL497: We set $\\tau=1$ (part (i)) or $\\tau=\\frac{1}{2(\\gamma+1)}$ (part (ii)) in Theorem cite42†4.2 . ∎\nL498: Note that by letting $\\gamma=0$ (i.e. equispaced points) we recover the original impossibility theorem (Theorem cite51†2.1 ). It is of interest to note that geometric convergence necessarily implies geometrically large condition numbers, regardless of the endpoint behaviour of the sampling points whenever $\\gamma=\\max\\{\\alpha,\\beta\\}>-1/2$. Conversely, this result says nothing about points that cluster quadratically or faster at $x=\\pm 1$, which corresponds to the case $-1<\\gamma\\leq-1/2$.\nL499: Indeed we shall prove in the next section that geometric convergence is possible in this setting with a stable approximation.\nL500: ## 5 Optimality of the approximation rate and discrete least squares\nL501: Theorem cite42†4.2 gives a necessary relation between the rate of exponential convergence and the rate of exponential ill-conditioning. For example, as asserted in Corollary cite96†4.3 , stable approximation necessarily implies subgeometric convergence with index $\\frac{1}{2(\\gamma+1)}$. We now show that there exists an algorithm that achieves these rates: namely, polynomial least-squares fitting. In particular, if the polynomial degree is chosen as\nL502:  | $$N\\asymp M^{\\nu},\\hskip 20.00003pt\\nu=\\frac{1}{2(\\gamma+1)},$$  |\nL503: \nL504: one obtains a stable approximation which converges exponentially with rate $\\frac{1}{2(\\gamma+1)}$.\nL505: ### 5.1 A sufficient condition for boundedness of the maximal polynomial\nL506: \nL507: We commence with a sufficient condition for boundedness of the quantity $B(M,N)$:\nL508: \nL509: ###### Lemma 5.1.\nL510: \nL511: Let $-1=x_{0}<\\ldots<x_{M}=1$ be arbitrary and suppose that $N\\zeta<1$, where\nL512: \nL513:  | $$\\zeta=\\max_{m=0,\\ldots,M-1}\\int^{x_{m+1}}_{x_{m}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.$$  |  | (5.1)\nL514: \nL515: If $B(M,N)$ is as in (cite67†3.1 ), then\nL516: \nL517:  | $$B(M,N)\\leq\\frac{1}{1-N\\zeta}.$$  |\nL518: ###### Proof.\nL519: \nL520: Let $p\\in\\mathbb{P}_{N}$ with $|p(x_{m})|\\leq 1$, $m=0,\\ldots,M$ and suppose that $-1\\leq x\\leq 1$ with $x_{m}\\leq x\\leq x_{m+1}$ for some $m=0,\\ldots,M-1$. Then\nL521: \nL522:  | $$|p(x)|\\leq|p(x_{m})|+\\int^{x}_{x_{m}}|p^{\\prime}(z)|\\,\\mathrm{d}z\\leq 1+\\sup_{-1\\leq z\\leq 1}|\\sqrt{1-z^{2}}p^{\\prime}(z)|\\int^{x_{m+1}}_{x_{m}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-z^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}z.$$  |\nL523: Bernstein’s inequality states that $|\\sqrt{1-z^{2}}p^{\\prime}(z)|\\leq N\\|p\\|_{\\infty}$ for any $-1\\leq z\\leq 1$ [cite90†11 , Thm. 5.1.7]. Hence\nL524: \nL525:  | $$|p(x)|\\leq 1+N\\zeta\\|p\\|_{\\infty}.$$  |\nL526: \nL527: Since $-1\\leq x\\leq 1$ was arbitrary the result now follows. ∎\nL528: \nL529: Note that this lemma makes no assumption on the points $\\{x_{m}\\}^{M}_{m=0}$. The following result estimates the constant $\\zeta$ for points arising from modified Jacobi weight functions:\nL530: ###### Lemma 5.2.\nL531: \nL532: Let $\\mu$ be a modified Jacobi weight function (cite94†2.8 ) with parameters $\\alpha,\\beta>-1$. If $\\zeta$ is as in (cite97†5.1 ), then\nL533: \nL534:  | $$\\zeta\\leq CM^{-\\frac{1}{2(1+\\gamma)}},$$  |\nL535: \nL536: where $\\gamma=\\max\\{\\alpha,\\beta,-1/2\\}$ and $C>0$ is a constant depending on $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ only.\nL537: ###### Proof.\nL538: \nL539: We consider the following four cases:\nL540: \nL541:   * (i)\nL542: \nL543: $-1<\\alpha,\\beta\\leq-1/2$,\nL544: \nL545:   * (ii)\nL546: \nL547: $-1<\\alpha\\leq-1/2$, $\\beta>-1/2$,\nL548: \nL549:   * (iii)\nL550: \nL551: $\\alpha>-1/2$, $-1<\\beta\\leq-1/2$,\nL552: \nL553:   * (iv)\nL554: \nL555: $\\alpha,\\beta>-1/2$.\nL556: \nL557: Case (i): Suppose first that $-1<\\alpha,\\beta\\leq-1/2$. Then\nL558: \nL559:  | $$\\int^{x_{m+1}}_{x_{m}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\lesssim\\int^{x_{m+1}}_{x_{m}}\\mu(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{1}{M}.$$  |\nL560: \nL561: Hence $\\zeta\\lesssim 1/M$ in this case.\nL562: Case (ii): Now suppose that $-1<\\alpha\\leq-1/2$ and $\\beta>-1/2$. Then\nL563: \nL564:  | $$\\int^{x_{m+1}}_{x_{m}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\lesssim\\int^{x_{m+1}}_{x_{m}}g(x)(1-x)^{\\alpha}(1+x)^{-1/2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.$$  |\nL565: \nL566: Recall from (cite80†3.10 ) that\nL567: \nL568:  | $$1+x_{m}\\gtrsim\\left(\\frac{m}{M}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{1+\\beta}},\\hskip 20.00003ptm=0,\\ldots,M,$$  |\nL569: \nL570: and therefore $1+x_{1}\\gtrsim M^{-\\frac{1}{1+\\beta}}$. For $m=1,\\ldots,M-1$ we have\nL571:  | $\\displaystyle\\int^{x_{m+1}}_{x_{m}}g(x)(1-x)^{\\alpha}(1+x)^{-1/2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$  | $\\displaystyle\\leq(1+x_{m})^{-1/2-\\beta}\\int^{x_{m+1}}_{x_{m}}g(x)(1-x)^{\\alpha}(1+x)^{\\beta}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$  |\nL572:  |  | $\\displaystyle=(1+x_{m})^{-1/2-\\beta}\\frac{1}{M}$  |\nL573:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\leq(1+x_{1})^{-1/2-\\beta}\\frac{1}{M}$  |\nL574:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\lesssim M^{-\\frac{1}{2(1+\\beta)}}.$  |\nL575: \nL576: Now let $m=0$. For this, we first notice that $x_{1}\\leq 0$ whenever $M\\gtrsim 1$. Therefore, we have\nL577:  | $$\\int^{x_{1}}_{x_{0}}g(x)(1-x)^{\\alpha}(1+x)^{-1/2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\lesssim\\sqrt{1+x_{1}}\\lesssim M^{-\\frac{1}{2(1+\\beta)}}.$$  |\nL578: \nL579: Hence we deduce that $\\zeta\\lesssim M^{-\\frac{1}{2(1+\\beta)}}$ for this case as well.\nL580: \nL581: Case (iii): This is identical to the previous case and thus omitted.\nL582: \nL583: Case (iv): For $m=1,\\ldots,M-2$ we have\nL584:  | $\\displaystyle\\int^{x_{m+1}}_{x_{m}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$  | $\\displaystyle\\leq(1-x_{m+1})^{-1/2-\\alpha}(1+x_{m})^{-1/2-\\beta}\\int^{x_{m+1}}_{x_{m}}g(x)(1-x)^{\\alpha}(1+x)^{\\beta}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$  |\nL585:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\leq(1-x_{M-1})^{-1/2-\\alpha}(1+x_{1})^{-1/2-\\beta}\\frac{1}{M}$  |\nL586:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\lesssim M^{1-\\frac{1}{2(1+\\alpha)}-\\frac{1}{2(1+\\beta)}}$  |\nL587:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\leq M^{-\\frac{1}{2(1+\\gamma)}},$  |\nL588: where in the final step we use the fact that $\\alpha,\\beta>-1/2$. For $m=0$, recalling that $x_{1}\\leq 0$ for all large $M$, we have\nL589: \nL590:  | $$\\int^{x_{1}}_{x_{0}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\lesssim\\sqrt{1+x_{1}}\\lesssim M^{-\\frac{1}{2(1+\\beta)}},$$  |\nL591: \nL592: and similarly for $m=M-1$, noting that $x_{M-1}\\geq 0$ for all large $M$ gives\nL593: \nL594:  | $$\\int^{x_{M}}_{x_{M-1}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\lesssim\\sqrt{1-x_{M}}\\lesssim M^{-\\frac{1}{2(1+\\alpha)}}.$$  |\nL595: We therefore deduce that $\\zeta\\lesssim M^{-\\frac{1}{2(1+\\gamma)}}$ as required. ∎\nL596: \nL597: This lemma immediately leads to the follow result:\nL598: ###### Proposition 5.3 (Necessary and sufficient condition for boundedness of $B(M,N)$).\nL599: \nL600: For $N,M\\in\\mathbb{N}$, let $\\{x_{m}\\}^{M}_{m=0}$ be equidistributed according to a modified Jacobi weight function (cite94†2.8 ) with parameters $\\alpha,\\beta>-1$. Then $B(M,N)\\lesssim 1$ if and only if\nL601: \nL602:  | $$M\\asymp N^{2(\\gamma+1)}.$$  |\nL603: ###### Proof.\nL604: \nL605: We combine Corollary cite84†3.2 and Lemma cite98†5.2 . ∎\nL606: The proposition is illustrated in Figure cite99†5 for cases (C2), (U), (UC) and (OC). It plots the smallest values of $M$ such that $B(M,N)\\leq 10$ for given values of $N$. Notice that the relationship between the computed values of $M$ and $N$ is in good agreement with the asymptotic relation $M\\asymp N^{2(\\gamma+1)}$. The constants used to define the dashed lines in this figure were chosen by trial and error.\nL607: Similar agreement is shown in Figure cite100†6 , where the contour levels of $B(M,N)$ for cases (U), (UC), and (OC) are presented. Notice that for the (OC) case $B(M,N)$ remains bounded with $M=CN$, but its values very quickly increase from 10 to more than $10^{13}$ when $C$ is decreased below $1.65$.\nL608: cite101†Image: Refer to caption L609: \nL610: Figure 5: Loglog plot of $N$ and $M$, where $M$ is the smallest integer such that $B(M,N)\\leq 10$. Circle markers show the computed values and dashed lines represent the theoretical estimate in Proposition cite102†5.3 . The dashed lines corresponding to (U), (C2) and (UC) are given by $M=(N/3)^{2(\\gamma+1)}$, while the dashed line corresponding to (OC) is $M=1.65N$.\nL611: \nL612: (U)             (UC)              (OC)\nL613: cite103†Image: Refer to caption L635: ###### Proof.\nL636: \nL637: Proposition cite105†2.4 gives $\\kappa(F_{M,N})\\leq\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{M}B(M,N)$ and\nL638: \nL639:  | $$\\|f-F_{M,N}(f)\\|_{\\infty}\\leq 2\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{M}B(M,N)\\inf_{p\\in\\mathbb{P}_{N}}\\|f-p\\|_{\\infty}.$$  |\nL640: \nL641: The result follows immediately from Proposition cite102†5.3 and the well-known error bound $\\inf_{p\\in\\mathbb{P}_{N}}\\|f-p\\|_{\\infty}\\leq\\frac{2}{\\theta-1}\\|f\\|_{E_{\\theta}}\\theta^{-N}$ (see, for example, [cite52†31 , Thm. 8.2]). ∎\nL642: ### 5.3 The mock-Chebyshev heuristic\nL643: \nL644: A well-known heuristic is that stable approximation from a set of $M+1$ points $-1=x_{0}<x_{1}<\\ldots<x_{M}=1$ is only possible once there exists a subset of $N+1$ of those points that mimic a Chebyshev grid (see, for example, [cite47†12 ]). We now confirm this heuristic. Let\nL645: \nL646:  | $$z_{n}=-\\cos(n\\pi/N),\\hskip 20.00003ptn=0,\\ldots,N,$$  |  | (5.2)\nL647: be a Chebyshev grid (note that the $z_{n}$ are equidistributed according to the Chebyshev weight function $\\mu(x)=\\frac{1}{\\pi\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}}$). Then we have:\nL648: ###### Proposition 5.6.\nL649: \nL650: Let $-1=x_{0}<\\ldots<x_{M}=1$ be arbitrary and suppose that\nL651: \nL652:  | $$N\\zeta<\\pi,$$  |\nL653: \nL654: where $\\zeta$ is as in (cite97†5.1 ). Then there exists a subset $\\{x^{*}_{n}\\}^{N}_{n=1}$ of $\\{x_{m}\\}^{M}_{m=0}$ such that\nL655: \nL656:  | $$-1=z_{0}<x^{*}_{1}<z_{1}<x^{*}_{2}<\\ldots<z_{N-1}<x^{*}_{N}<z_{N}=1,$$  |  | (5.3)\nL657: where the $z_{n}$ are as in (cite106†5.2 ). In particular, if $\\{x_{m}\\}^{M}_{m=0}$ are equidistributed according to a modified Jacobi weight function with parameters $\\alpha,\\beta>-1$ then there exists such a subset $\\{x^{*}_{n}\\}^{N}_{n=1}$ whenever\nL658: \nL659:  | $$M\\asymp N^{2(\\gamma+1)},\\hskip 20.00003pt\\gamma=\\max\\{\\alpha,\\beta,-1/2\\}.$$  |\nL660: ###### Proof.\nL661: \nL662: Let $\\theta_{m}=\\cos^{-1}(-x_{m})\\in[0,\\pi]$ so that\nL663: \nL664:  | $$\\theta_{m+1}-\\theta_{m}=\\int^{x_{m+1}}_{x_{m}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\leq\\zeta<\\pi/N.$$  |  | (5.4)\nL665: \nL666: We now construct a subset $\\{\\phi_{n}\\}^{N}_{n=1}\\subseteq\\{\\theta_{m}\\}^{M}_{m=0}$ such that\nL667: \nL668:  | $$0<\\phi_{1}<\\frac{\\pi}{N}<\\phi_{2}<\\frac{2\\pi}{N}<\\ldots<\\frac{(N-1)\\pi}{N}<\\phi_{N}<\\pi.$$  |\nL669: First, let $m_{1}$ be the largest $m$ such that $\\theta_{m}<\\pi/N$. Set $\\phi_{1}=\\theta_{m_{1}}$. Next, observe that $\\theta_{m_{1}+1}<\\theta_{m_{1}}+\\pi/N<2\\pi/N$. Hence there exists at least one of the $\\theta_{m}$’s in the interval $(\\pi/N,2\\pi/N)$. Let $m_{2}$ be the largest $m$ such that $\\theta_{m}<2\\pi/N$ and set $\\phi_{2}=\\theta_{m_{2}}$. We now continue in the same way to construct a sequence $\\{\\phi_{n}\\}^{N}_{n=1}$ with the required property.\nL670: Since the function $\\cos^{-1}(-\\theta)$ is increasing on $[0,\\pi]$ it follows that the sequence $\\{x^{*}_{n}\\}^{N}_{n=1}$ with $x^{*}_{n}=\\cos^{-1}(-\\phi_{n})$ satisfies (cite107†5.3 ). ∎\nL671: Recalling Lemma cite108†5.1 , we note that the same sufficient condition (up to a small change in the right-hand side) for boundedness of the maximal polynomial (for arbitrary points) also guarantees an interlacing property of a subset of $N$ of those points with the Chebyshev nodes. In particular, if $M,N\\rightarrow\\infty$ with $N\\zeta\\leq c$, where $c<\\pi$, the nodes $\\{x^{*}_{n}\\}^{N}_{n=1}$ equidistribute according to the Chebyshev weight function $\\mu(x)=\\frac{1}{\\pi\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}}$.\nL672: Moreover, for modified Jacobi weight functions the sampling rate that guarantees the existence of this ‘mock-Chebyshev’ grid, i.e. $M\\asymp N^{2(\\gamma+1)}$, is identical to that which was found to be both necessary and sufficient for stable approximation via discrete least-squares (recall Proposition cite43†5.5 ).\nL673: ## 6 Computation of $B(M,N)$ and the maximal polynomial\nL674: \nL675: Let $-1=x_{0}<x_{1}<\\ldots<x_{M}=1$ be a set of $M+1$ points. We now introduce an algorithm for the computation of\nL676: \nL677:  | $$B(M,N)=\\max\\left\\{{\\left\\|p\\right\\|}_{\\infty}:p\\in\\mathbb{P}_{N},\\ |p(x_{m})|\\leq 1,\\ m=0,\\ldots,M\\right\\},$$  |\nL678: \nL679: and the maximizing polynomial $p\\in\\mathbb{P}_{N}$. In fact, in order to compute this polynomial we will first compute the pointwise quantity\nL680:  | $$B(M,N,x)=\\max\\left\\{|p(x)|:|p(x_{m})|\\leq 1,\\ m=0,\\ldots,M,\\ p\\in\\mathbb{P}_{N}\\right\\},\\hskip 10.00002pt-1\\leq x\\leq 1.$$  |  | (6.1)\nL681: \nL682: As we prove below, $B(M,N,x)$ is a piecewise polynomial with knots at the points $\\{x_{m}\\}^{M}_{m=0}$. Hence the maximal polynomial for $B(M,N)$ can be obtained by computing $B(M,N,x)$ in each subinterval and identifying the interval and corresponding polynomial in which the maximum is attained.\nL683: Our algorithm for computing (cite109†6.1 ) is a variant of the classical Remez algorithm for computing best uniform approximations (see, for example, [cite45†25 , cite46†28 ]). It is based on a result of Schönhage [cite44†30 ].\nL684: ### 6.1 Derivation\nL685: \nL686: We first require some notation. Given a set $Y=\\{y_{n}\\}^{N}_{n=0}$ of $N+1$ points, let\nL687: \nL688:  | $$\\ell_{Y,n}(x)=\\prod^{N}_{\\begin{subarray}{c}m=0\\\\\nL689: m\\neq n\\end{subarray}}\\frac{x-y_{m}}{y_{n}-y_{m}},\\hskip 20.00003ptn=0,\\ldots,N,$$  |\nL690: \nL691: be the Lagrange polynomials, and\nL692: \nL693:  | $$L_{Y}(x)=\\max\\left\\{|p(x)|:p\\in\\mathbb{P}_{N},|p(y_{n})|\\leq 1,n=0,\\ldots,N\\right\\}=\\sum^{N}_{n=0}|\\ell_{Y,n}(x)|,$$  |\n"

